A||||5||Motorhead||Air Conditioning
Z||000000||Motorhead||04-08-99||07:37 PM||||I bought my Fiero last month, and it seems the previous owner decided that A/C wasnt neccesary in a climate that averages 90 degrees 4 months out of the year!<br>Somewhere along the line, he elected not to include the entire A/C system aft of the firewall into his plans for engine overhaul!<p>SOOOO!!!  I find myself trying to bring my car up to original specs without a clue as to how the original A/C system was installed.<p>After cruising through the catalogs, seems as though the '86 had an A/C system that was unique among its counterparts.<p>What gives!<p>Am I going to have to find another '86 model to restore my A/C to its original, operating condition, or are the systems interchageable to some degree?<p>This car is missing everything aft of the firewall! But it has this silly switch labeled A/C on the climate control panel, along with all the associated components in the forward compartment under the "bonnet".<p>June in South Carolina is approaching fast!!  Any help, suggestions, prayers, PITTY, greatly appreciated!
Z||000001||theogre||04-08-99||10:43 PM||||Fiero AC is unique to all years. Items such as compressors, acum/dryer, orifice tube, etc are interchangable, but allot of piping isn't. I'm not sure the condenser, or evaporator, are unique.<p>Most parts you don't have can be fabricated, adapted, or purchased thru dealers/parts houses. Keep in mind handling R-12 without proper federal paperwork caries Major fines in the U.S. (I never know who lives where on this thing.) R-12 is also increadibly expensive. R-134 has problem of its own that are fairly well known, like very high operating preasure.<p>I'm looking into FridgeC from Penzoil, but so far I can't find anything about it other than penzoil's marketing data. They claim it's better than R134 for retrofit to pre90 cars.
Z||000002||Motorhead||04-09-99||02:38 AM||||Thanks Ogre. I've just about resigned myself to buying a compressor and fabricating the necessary brackets and hoses. <p>You touched on the other subject on my mind, refrigerant!<p>Dealing with the federal regulations and mountain of information concerning the affects of compounds on the atmosphere make me want to just roll the windows down and increase the cars speed as the ambient air temperature increases!<p>Keep us informed on what your doing to safely keep the sweat off your brow.
Z||000003||Bozone||04-10-99||10:33 PM||||Motorhead - Forgive me for asking what might be obvious, but why aren't you getting your parts off a same-year junker? I've got two 86GTs with defunct air conditioners and someday soon I'm going to have to tackle them.
Z||000004||Motorhead||04-11-99||06:23 AM||||Good question!<p>Seems that here in South Carolina Fieros' weren't real popular with the "good ole' boys". Consequently, parts cars are few and far between. I bought my Datsun Z parts car from Ohio. If your restoring a pick-up truck here, you've got it made.<p>I'd be really interested in buying the necessary components from you if your willing to sell.<p>I moved here from northern Virginia and am used to finding parts for even rare cars an easy task. I guess I'll restore an old Ford pick-up next!
Z||000005||Bozone||04-11-99||11:31 AM||||Motorhead - Yep, well I'm in the market for an 86GT junker. I mean it this time. The last one I found was too good and now I have 2 of them and need parts even more. But if I find one with air conditioning, I'll let you know. I'm in the northeast and there should be a lot of them around -- just haven't found one yet. Is that a GT you have?
